        "original_id": "c6caae25-44d5-5ec7-bc86-18448885093b",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Erich Schmidt-Leichner, Anneliese Michel\n- Roles: German lawyer, defense counsel\n- Events: Nuremberg Trials, Klingenberg Case, Catholic exorcism rites\n- Topics: negligent homicide, exorcism\n\nFacts:\n- Erich Schmidt-Leichner was born on 14 October 1910 and died on 17 March 1983.\n- Erich Schmidt-Leichner was a German lawyer who became known as a distinguished defense counsel at the Nuremberg Trials from 1945 to 1946.\n- Anneliese Michel was born on 21 September 1952 and died on 1 July 1976.\n- Anneliese Michel was a German woman who underwent Catholic exorcism rites during the year before her death.\n- In 1978, Erich Schmidt-Leichner served as defense counsel in the Klingenberg Case involving Anneliese Michel.\n- In the Klingenberg Case, a married couple were accused of negligent homicide for failing to call a medical doctor during an exorcism of their daughter.",
